<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    angr StackCanarySimplifier, surfaced by angr's
    test_ite_region_converter_missing_break_statement::authenticate
    (binaries/tests/x86_64/ite_region_converter_missing_breaks, x86-64 GCC,
    -fstack-protector).

    A function compiled with -fstack-protector saves the TLS canary at entry
    (canary = *(fs:0x28), stored to a stack slot) and, at every exit, reloads
    fs:0x28 and compares:

        if (canary_slot != *(fs:0x28)) __stack_chk_fail();   // no-return handler
        return v;

    The canary check is a SHARED single return point, so a return deep inside
    the loop (the `Authenticated!` path) cannot return directly - Ghidra's
    structurer routes it through a `goto` to the shared tail (plus a `label:`).
    Ghidra keeps the check verbatim; angr's StackCanarySimplifier strips it,
    after which the bare-return tail is duplicated by ActionReturnSplit into
    each predecessor and the goto disappears.  (angr renders the unrelated
    trailing if/else as a ternary `c ? 1 : 0`; Ghidra's C printer has no
    ternary operator, so that exact rendering is out of scope - the canary
    strip is the reproducible win.)

    Stage model: S7 region/structure recovery, "edge-virtualization" sub-stage
    (the goto is a forced edge).  The kuna Action runs late on the simplified
    post-SSA CFG, immediately before ActionReturnSplit; it removes the
    corrupted-canary branch with the stock Funcdata::removeBranch primitive and
    lets removeUnreachableBlocks collect the orphaned __stack_chk_fail block.
    Detection is purely structural (the canary compare: both CBRANCH operands
    derive from a LOAD of base+0x28, with a handler CALL on the corrupted
    branch) because kuna's BFD console loader does not resolve the PLT stub to
    its __stack_chk_fail name (it renders as sub_<addr>).

    P0 assertion: `option stackguard on|off` (default off = upstream
    byte-identical).  Only `authenticate` is decompiled; its bytes are
    self-contained (callees resolve to unnamed sub_<addr>, exactly as in the
    full ELF).

    Pass 1 (option stackguard off): assertions prove the bug - the
    __stack_chk_fail call (sub_10e0) is rendered AND a goto/label is emitted.
    Pass 2 (option stackguard on): assertions prove the fix - no
    __stack_chk_fail call, no goto, and the deep match path returns directly.

    GH-183 (entry-side canary init): stripping the check must also remove the
    entry-side canary init store (canary_slot = *(fs:0x28)) and its TLS-base
    residue - angr's StackCanarySimplifier pops exactly that statement.
    Assertions 5 and 6 span BOTH passes' concatenated output: the two fs+0x28
    accesses (entry init + epilogue reload) and the one fs_offset declaration
    all belong to the option-off pass; the option-on pass renders no canary
    residue at all (before GH-183 the counts were 3 and 2).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x22e5">

</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x22e5" name="authenticate"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <!-- returndup (DIV-54 default-on) also inlines the deep match path as a direct
       return, so turn it off to keep stackguard the only difference between passes. -->
  <com>option returndup off</com>
  <com>option stackguard off</com>
  <com>lo fu authenticate</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option stackguard on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="angr-StackGuard #1: bug -- __stack_chk_fail call (sub_10e0) rendered only with option off" min="1" max="1">sub_10e0\(\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="angr-StackGuard #2: bug -- a goto is emitted only with option off" min="1" max="1">goto </stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="angr-StackGuard #3: bug -- label (goto target + definition) emitted only with option off" min="2" max="2">label_</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="angr-StackGuard #4: fix -- only the option-on pass inlines the deep match path as a direct return 1" min="1" max="1">return 1;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="angr-StackGuard #5: fix (GH-183) -- the fs+0x28 canary accesses (entry init + reload) survive only in the option-off pass" min="2" max="2">\+ 0x28\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="angr-StackGuard #6: fix (GH-183) -- the TLS-base fs_offset declaration survives only in the option-off pass" min="1" max="1">fs_offset</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
